  "number": 7044,
  "checked_at": "2026-09-21T11:55:57.937720+00:00",
  "title": "Keep Thunderbolt USB-C xHCI awake on Intel MacBooks",
  "author": "shawnyeager",
  "head_sha": "65131c9b8d98b61bf69f641b41ace50800f7a5a0",
  "base_sha": "b9ddccfc377abe0b8fc3ff1ee5b31a86bf202d4a",
  "base_ref": "dev",
  "updated_at": "2026-08-15T23:51:34Z",
  "state": "open",
  "body": "## Summary\r\n\r\nOn Intel MacBooks whose USB-C ports sit behind an Intel JHL6540 Thunderbolt 3 xHCI controller (`8086:15d4`), the controller runtime-suspends into D3cold after a device is unplugged. The next plug-in never enumerates \u2014 no kernel USB event, no `/dev/sd*`, nothing for udiskie to mount \u2014 until `xhci_hcd` is rebound.\r\n\r\nThis is separate from `usbcore.autosuspend` (already disabled by `usb-autosuspend.sh`). The PCI device itself is asleep.\r\n\r\n## Fix\r\n\r\n- New hardware script `install/config/hardware/apple/fix-tb-usb-wakeup.sh`\r\n- Installs a udev rule that sets `power/control=on` and `d3cold_allowed=0` on `8086:15d4`\r\n- Gated on Apple DMI + that PCI ID (2016\u20132017 MacBook Pro / Alpine Ridge)\r\n- Wired into `install/config/all.sh` for fresh installs\r\n- Migration `1786836656.sh` applies the same script on existing machines\r\n\r\n## Tested\r\n\r\nReproduced on Omarchy 4 / `MacBookPro14,3` (2017 15\" T1):\r\n\r\n- After unplugging a SanDisk USB-C drive, both TB xHCI devices showed `enable=0`, `runtime=suspended`\r\n- A second USB-C drive produced no kernel events until `power/control=on` and an xhci rebind\r\n- With the udev rule, the same drive enumerated immediately as `/dev/sda`\r\n\r\n## Notes\r\n\r\nT2 Macs use different Thunderbolt silicon and already have their own suspend path. This only matches JHL6540 (`8086:15d4`).\r\n",
